---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import StaticCode from "../../components/StaticCode.astro";
import { targets } from "../../lib/site";

const toc = [
  { id: "matrix", label: "Target matrix" },
  { id: "arithmetic", label: "Checked arithmetic" },
  { id: "projects", label: "Generated projects" },
  { id: "invariants", label: "Generated-code invariants" },
  { id: "names", label: "Portable names" },
  { id: "outcomes", label: "Execution outcomes" },
  { id: "toolchains", label: "Toolchain policy" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Targets"
  description="How h# generates C#, Rust, Go, and Java projects, checks arithmetic, maps names, and reports outcomes."
  active="/docs/targets"
  toc={toc}
  prev={{ href: "/docs/cli", label: "CLI" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/verification", label: "Verification" }}
>
  <h2 id="matrix">Target matrix</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Value type</th><th>Generated project</th><th>Build</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        targets.map((target) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{target.label}</strong></td>
            <td><code>{target.id === "go" ? "int32" : target.id === "rust" ? "i32" : "int"}</code></td>
            <td>{target.id === "csharp" ? "HsProgram.csproj + Program.cs" : target.id === "rust" ? "Cargo.toml + src/main.rs" : target.id === "go" ? "go.mod + main.go" : "HsProgram.java"}</td>
            <td><code>{target.command}</code></td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    C# targets <code>net10.0</code>; Go declares <code>go 1.22</code>; Rust uses edition 2021 and no
    external crates; Java uses no external libraries. Generated projects are standalone executables,
    not libraries or packages.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arithmetic">Checked arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Every backend implements one rule: widen i32 operands to signed 64-bit, perform the operation,
    check −2,147,483,648 through 2,147,483,647, then return a failure result or narrow the value.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`fn main(x: i32) -> i32 {
    return x + 1;
}`} language="hsharp" title="overflow.hs" meta="E_OVERFLOW at x = 2147483647" />
  <p>
    The failure is attached to the operation's source node and propagates through the internal
    result record. It is never silently wrapped. The reference interpreter uses BigInt and checks
    independently, so the backends are not validating themselves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="projects">Generated projects</h2>
  <div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">C#</div>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
        A <code>Res</code> readonly struct, <code>long</code> intermediates, static functions, and a
        <code>Main</code> that parses arguments and prints the outcome JSON.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Rust</div>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
        A <code>Res</code> struct with <code>ok</code>/<code>fail</code> constructors, <code>i64</code>
        intermediates, checked helpers, and no external crates.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Go</div>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
        <code>int32</code> values with <code>int64</code> checks, a <code>Res</code> struct, and
        explicit <code>ok</code>/<code>fail</code> functions.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Java</div>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
        <code>int</code> values with <code>long</code> checks, a nested <code>Res</code> class, and
        <code>System.exit</code> reflecting the outcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="invariants">Generated-code invariants</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No source identifier reaches generated code. Functions and locals become compiler-assigned IDs.</li>
    <li>Each target carries its own internal result type and checked arithmetic helpers.</li>
    <li>Every arithmetic operation widens, checks the i32 range, then narrows or fails.</li>
    <li>Generated programs print exactly one outcome line and no extra runtime logging.</li>
    <li>No backend requires an external package, framework, or runtime beyond its standard toolchain.</li>
    <li>Generated projects are standalone executables, not reusable libraries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="names">Portable names</h2>
  <p>
    Every function and local receives a compiler-assigned ID: <code>f1</code>, <code>f2</code>,
    <code>v1</code>, <code>v2</code>. Source identifiers never reach generated code. An h# name
    such as <code>int</code>, <code>class</code>, or <code>string</code> cannot collide with a host
    keyword.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`fn int(class: i32) -> i32 {
    let string: i32 = class + 1;
    return string;
}`} language="hsharp" title="target_names.hs" meta="generated as f1/v1" />

  <h2 id="outcomes">Execution outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    Generated programs print exactly one machine-readable line on stdout. Build logs stay separate.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={`{"status":"ok","type":"i32","value":32}
{"status":"error","code":"E_OVERFLOW","node":17}`} language="json" title="outcomes" />
  <p>
    A success exits 0. A program failure exits non-zero. Invalid harness arguments produce
    <code>&#123;"status":"badargs"&#125;</code>. The conformance runner checks the outcome, the failure node,
    and the process status.
  </p>

  <h2 id="toolchains">Toolchain policy</h2>
  <p>
    Toolchain detection is separate from program verification. An older or missing .NET SDK, Go,
    Rust, or Java marks the target <strong>blocked</strong> and makes the suite exit 2. It never
    becomes a passing row. C# requires .NET 10 because generated projects target
    <code>net10.0</code>; Go requires 1.22.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
